---
// "Position size and risk of ruin" as served (definition.md beside this file): how the game works, two open numbered
// steps in one column, then the bars for every size at the same odds and the lesson. The page opens on the coin game's
// odds (60%, even money) at a 10% size, printed in full by the functions the block script imports
// (position-size-view.mjs), so the script only rewrites what is already here. Step 1 leads with the runs chart (the
// kit's, _kit/runs-chart.mjs): thirty drawn paths under the exact-odds sentence, one slider for the size. The slider,
// Replay, "Try another example" and the two typed boxes are hidden until the script runs them; with script off the
// opening example, its paths and tally, its bars and the lesson read as a page of their own.
import { DEFAULT_EXAMPLE, DEFAULT_SIZE, EXAMPLES, SIZE_MAX, SIZE_MIN, outcomes, runOdds } from "./position-size.mjs";
import { FLOOR, START, answerHtml, barsHtml, chartParts, edgeExplain, firstTradeText, kellyText, payoffNote, sayText, share, sliderText, winNote, winThenLoss } from "./position-size-view.mjs";

const ex = EXAMPLES[DEFAULT_EXAMPLE];
const r = runOdds(ex.win, ex.payoff);
if (!r.ok) throw new Error("the opening example must be valid");
const sl = sliderText(DEFAULT_SIZE);
const chart = chartParts(r, DEFAULT_SIZE);
const at = (s: number) => share(outcomes(r, s).floor);
---
<div data-ruin>
  <p class="words"><b>How it works.</b> You start with {START}. You make 100 trades in a row. Each trade wins or loses at fixed odds: here each trade has a {ex.win}% chance to win, so about {ex.win} trades in 100 win, and a win pays what the trade risked.</p>
  <p class="words">You choose one number: the share of the account each trade risks. That is your <b>size</b>. The trade's dollar amount changes as the account grows or shrinks; the share stays the same. Everything else is luck.</p>
  <p class="words">One set of 100 trades is a <b>run</b>. The page works out every possible run and how likely each one is. Then it counts two things: the runs that end above {START}, and the runs that fall to {FLOOR}, half the start, at some point. A run that falls to {FLOOR} stops there. That is what this page calls <b>ruin</b>.</p>
  <p class="words">The <b>edge</b> is what a trade earns on average for each $1 it risks. {edgeExplain(r)}</p>
  <section class="blk calc" aria-labelledby="pr-h1">
    <h2 class="lab" id="pr-h1" data-js hidden>Step 1 · Choose how much each trade risks</h2>
    <p class="lab" data-nojs>The example: a {ex.win}% win chance, even money, {DEFAULT_SIZE}% of the account on each trade</p>
    <p class="tsent" data-say aria-live="polite">{sayText(r, DEFAULT_SIZE)}</p>
    <div class="rc" data-chart>
      <svg class="rc-svg rc-wide" viewBox="0 0 752 300" role="img" aria-label={chart.label} data-view="wide" set:html={chart.wide}></svg>
      <svg class="rc-svg rc-narrow" viewBox="0 0 358 230" role="img" aria-label={chart.label} data-view="narrow" set:html={chart.narrow}></svg>
    </div>
    <div data-js hidden>
      <div class="rc-ctl">
        <div class="rc-rw">
          <input type="range" class="rng" id="pr-size" aria-label="Share of the account each trade risks" min={SIZE_MIN} max={SIZE_MAX} step="1" value={sl.value} aria-valuetext={sl.valuetext} />
          <i class="rc-tick" data-tick hidden></i>
        </div>
        <div class="rc-side"><span class="rc-rd" data-read>{DEFAULT_SIZE}%</span><button class="btn" type="button" data-replay>Replay</button></div>
      </div>
      <p class="pdesc rc-note" data-tick-note></p>
    </div>
    <p class="rc-tally" data-tally aria-live="polite">{chart.tally}</p>
    <div class="sd-ans" data-ans aria-live="polite" set:html={answerHtml(r, DEFAULT_SIZE)}></div>
    <p class="pdesc" data-first aria-live="polite">{firstTradeText(r, DEFAULT_SIZE)}</p>
    <div data-js hidden><div class="row"><button class="btn" type="button" data-another>Try another example</button></div></div>
    <p class="stamp"><span><b>As of</b> your numbers</span><span><b>Source</b> computed on this page. Sample odds, not anyone's record.</span></p>
  </section>
  <section class="blk calc" aria-labelledby="pr-h2" data-js hidden>
    <h2 class="lab" id="pr-h2">Step 2 · Change the odds</h2>
    <p class="pdesc">Everything above and below follows these two numbers.</p>
    <div class="plate">
      <div class="sd-fields">
        <div class="sd-field" data-field="win">
          <label for="pr-win">Chance a trade wins</label>
          <div class="in"><input id="pr-win" type="text" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" value={String(ex.win)} aria-describedby="pr-win-n" aria-invalid="false" /><span aria-hidden="true">%</span></div>
          <small id="pr-win-n">{winNote(r)}</small>
        </div>
        <div class="sd-field" data-field="payoff">
          <label for="pr-pay">What a win pays, as a % of what the trade risked</label>
          <div class="in"><input id="pr-pay" type="text" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" value={String(ex.payoff)} aria-describedby="pr-pay-n" aria-invalid="false" /><span aria-hidden="true">%</span></div>
          <small id="pr-pay-n">{payoffNote(r)}</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lab">Every size, the same odds</p>
    <p class="pdesc">Each row is one size. Green is the share of runs that end above {START}. Red is the share that fall to {FLOOR} and stop. The grey rest end in between.</p>
    <div class="sim-bars" data-bars set:html={barsHtml(r, DEFAULT_SIZE)}></div>
    <div class="lg" style="margin-top:12px">
      <span><svg width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="14" height="14" fill="var(--gain)"/></svg>ended above {START}</span>
      <span><svg width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="14" height="14" fill="var(--loss)"/></svg>fell to {FLOOR} and stopped</span>
      <span><svg width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="14" height="14" fill="color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 14%,var(--plate))"/></svg>ended between {FLOOR} and {START}</span>
    </div>
    <p class="stamp"><span><b>As of</b> your numbers</span><span><b>Source</b> computed on this page, the exact odds over every possible run, no simulation</span></p>
  </section>
  <section class="blk teach">
    <p class="lab">What it shows</p>
    <p class="big">The odds are the same in every row. Only the size changes, and the size decides how the runs end.</p>
    <p>Why: a loss hurts more than a win of the same size helps. Win 10% and then lose 10%, and {START} becomes {winThenLoss(10)}. At 50%, a win and then a loss leave {winThenLoss(50)}. The bigger the size, the bigger that leak.</p>
    <p>So one size grows the account fastest: the edge divided by the payoff. At these odds it is {kellyText(r.kelly)}. Smaller sizes grow more slowly but rarely fall to {FLOOR}: at {DEFAULT_SIZE}%, {at(10)} of runs do. Bigger sizes take more risk and grow more slowly too: at 40%, {at(40)} of runs fall to {FLOOR}. Even at {kellyText(r.kelly)}, {at(20)} of runs fall to {FLOOR} at some point on the way.</p>
    <p>Traders call this question position sizing.</p>
  </section>
</div>
